title: 'Introducing GetHired for Companies: An AI Recruiter for Startup Tech Hiring'
publishedAt: 2026-10-21
type: companies
---
**Quick answer:** GetHired for Companies is a hiring workspace for startups and small tech teams. You post a role, GetHired ranks matched candidates from its talent pool and explains why each one fits, every applicant gets an AI fit score, and a recruiter agent can do most of the work from one chat. It also includes a pipeline board, consent-based outreach, AI screening interviews, a branded careers page and team roles. You can [start hiring free](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/auth/sign-up?company=true).

For most founders, hiring the first ten engineers looks the same. You post a job on three boards, get two hundred applications, skim resumes at midnight, copy promising names into a spreadsheet and send cold emails that nobody answers. The tools built for this are made for recruiting teams at large companies. They're expensive, slow to set up and assume someone's job is to run them.

We built the company side of GetHired for the team that doesn't have a recruiter yet.

## What a hiring process looks like on GetHired

Hiring comes down to five steps:

1. Post a job.
2. Find candidates.
3. Decide who's worth a conversation.
4. Contact them.
5. Run the process through to a hire.

Most job boards help with the first step and part of the second. Everything after that happens in email threads and spreadsheets. GetHired now covers all five.

### 1. Post a job

Create a job post from a form, or ask the agent: "Create a job post for a founding backend engineer, Go and Postgres, remote in India, 30 to 40 LPA." The agent drafts the post and shows it to you before anything goes live.

Each post can carry a custom application form with typed questions and knockout rules, and it shows up on your own careers page. More on that in [our guide to careers pages and application forms](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/build-a-branded-careers-page-and-custom-application-form-in-minutes).

### 2. Find candidates

As soon as a job is live, GetHired matches it against candidates who have made their profiles public. Matching uses semantic search rather than keyword counting, then an AI re-ranking step, so a candidate who "built event pipelines on Kafka" can match a job asking for "experience with distributed systems".

Every matched candidate gets a **match score from 0 to 100** and a short explanation: strengths, gaps and any conflicts, such as "wants remote; this job is onsite in Pune". You see why someone is on the list, not just that they are.

### 3. Decide who's worth a conversation

Applications to your jobs are screened in the background. Each one gets a **fit score**, a recommendation, a short summary, a check against each must-have requirement with evidence from the resume, and feedback on the screening answers. You can sort a list of two hundred applicants by fit and start at the top. Our post on [AI applicant screening](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-applicant-screening-how-to-sort-200-applicants-by-fit-without-reading-them-all) explains how the score is built.

If you want more signal before a call, turn on an **AI screening interview** for the job. Applicants above your bar are invited to a short, structured voice interview, and you get a one-page report scored against your own competencies. See [AI screening interviews](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/ai-screening-interviews-replace-30-phone-screens-with-one-page-reports).

### 4. Contact them

From a candidate's profile you can send an invite to apply or a note asking if they're open to a chat. The candidate answers **Interested** or **Not interested** in their GetHired dashboard. Their contact details are shared with you only after they say yes or apply, which is why reply rates are higher than with cold email: every candidate you reach chose to be found. Read more in [consent-based outreach](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-find-and-contact-candidates-who-want-to-be-found-consent-based-outreach).

### 5. Run the process to a hire

Every job has a pipeline with stages you can rename and reorder (Applied, Screening, Interview, Offer, Hired, plus Rejected and On hold by default). Drag candidates on a board, move them in bulk, leave notes and ratings, and send stage emails from templates that include your scheduling link. A funnel shows where each role stands. See [a lightweight ATS for startups](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/a-lightweight-ats-for-startups-pipeline-stages-boards-notes-and-email-templates).

## The recruiter agent

Everything above is available as buttons and pages. It's also available in a chat.

The GetHired recruiter agent knows your company, your open jobs and their applicants. You can ask it to:

- write or update a job post;
- find the best candidates for a role and explain each match;
- compare your top three candidates side by side;
- summarise new applicants and move them between stages;
- draft and send outreach;
- invite applicants to an AI screening interview;
- invite a cofounder or hiring manager to your team.

Anything that changes something outside the chat, such as publishing a post, emailing a candidate or moving an application, shows an approval card with the exact content first. Nothing happens until you approve it. The full tour is in [Meet the GetHired recruiter agent](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/meet-the-gethired-recruiter-agent-hire-tech-talent-from-one-chat).

## Built for small teams, ready for bigger ones

When the second person joins your hiring, invite them. Owners have full access, and you can create custom roles from a list of permissions. A hiring manager, for example, can be limited to the jobs they're assigned to. An audit log records who changed what. See [hiring as a team](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/hiring-as-a-team-custom-roles-permissions-and-hiring-teams-on-gethired).

## AI assists, people decide

Every score and summary in GetHired is labelled as AI-generated. The AI never rejects anyone on its own, and it never sees names, photos, age, gender or other protected characteristics when it scores a candidate. The agent refuses to filter by them. We explain the safeguards in [fair AI hiring on GetHired](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/fair-ai-hiring-how-gethired-keeps-bias-out-of-candidate-screening).

## Pricing

You can post a job, see matched candidates and screen applicants for free. Paid plans add more job posts, team seats, outreach volume and AI interviews, and usage beyond your plan's allowance is paid with credits. Current plans are on the [pricing page](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/pricing/companies).

## Get started

1. [Create a company account](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/auth/sign-up?company=true) and finish onboarding.
2. Post your first job, or ask the agent to draft it.
3. Open the job's matched candidates and applicants.

If you're curious how it looks before signing up, the [GetHired for hiring page](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/hire) has a live demo of the agent.
